Source for Acupuncture Method, Vasculature, Innervation, and Indications:
D. Liangyue, G. Yijun, H. Shuhui, et al. Chinese Acupuncture and Moxibustion. Revised ed. Beijing: Foreign Languages Press; 1999. Chapter 9, Acupuncture Points of the Jueyin and Shaoyang Meridian. ISBN 978-7-119-01758-7.</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
        <TARA:hasChineseLabel>天衝(冲)</TARA:hasChineseLabel>
        <TARA:hasIndicationsDescription>Headache, epilepsy, swelling and pain of the gums, convulsion.</TARA:hasIndicationsDescription>
        <TARA:hasInnervationDescription>The branch of the great occipital nerve.</TARA:hasInnervationDescription>
        <TARA:hasLocationalDescription>On the head, directly superior to the posterior border of the auricular root, 2 B-cun superior to the hairline. Note: GB9 is 0.5 B-cun posterior to GB8.</TARA:hasLocationalDescription>
