Source for Acupuncture Method, Vasculature, Innervation, and Indications:
D. Liangyue, G. Yijun, H. Shuhui, et al. Chinese Acupuncture and Moxibustion. Revised ed. Beijing: Foreign Languages Press; 1999. Chapter 7, Acupuncture Points of the Taiyin and Yangming Meridian. ISBN 978-7-119-01758-7.</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
        <TARA:hasChineseLabel>巨骨(骨)</TARA:hasChineseLabel>
        <TARA:hasIndicationsDescription>Pain and motor impairment of the upper extremities, pain in the shoulder and back.</TARA:hasIndicationsDescription>
        <TARA:hasInnervationDescription>Superficially, the lateral supraclavicular nerve, the branch of the accessory nerve; deeper, the suprascapular nerve.</TARA:hasInnervationDescription>
        <TARA:hasLocationalDescription>On the shoulder girdle, in the depression between the acromial end of the clavicle and the spine of the scapula. Note: In the depression between the two bones lateral to the suprascapular fossa.</TARA:hasLocationalDescription>
        <TARA:hasMeridian rdf:resource="http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_4610474"/>
        <TARA:hasMethodDescription>Puncture perpendicularly 0.5-0.7 inch. Moxibustion is applicable.</TARA:hasMethodDescription>
        <TARA:hasPinyinLabel>Jugu</TARA:hasPinyinLabel>
        <TARA:hasRelatedLocation rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/UBERON_0001105"/>
        <TARA:hasRelatedLocation rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/UBERON_0006849"/>
        <TARA:hasRelatedLocation rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/UBERON_4200020"/>
        <TARA:hasSurfaceLocation rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/UBERON_0001421"/>
        <TARA:hasSynonym>Large Intestine 16</TARA:hasSynonym>
        <TARA:hasVasculatureDescription>Deeper, the suprascapular artery and vein.</TARA:hasVasculatureDescription>
        <rdfs:label>LI 16</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


