        <dcterms:bibliographicCitation>Source for the Acupoint Location: WHO Standard Acupuncture Point Locations in the Western Pacific Region. World Health Organization. Regional Office for the Western Pacific, 2008. Page 97. ISBN 978-92-9061-248-7.

Source for Acupuncture Method, Vasculature, Innervation, and Indications:
D. Liangyue, G. Yijun, H. Shuhui, et al. Chinese Acupuncture and Moxibustion. Revised ed. Beijing: Foreign Languages Press; 1999. Chapter 8, Acupuncture Points of the Shaoyin and Taiyang Meridian. ISBN 978-7-119-01758-7.</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
        <TARA:hasChineseLabel>聽(听,聴)宮(宫)</TARA:hasChineseLabel>
        <TARA:hasIndicationsDescription>Deafness, tinnitus, otorrhea, motor impairment of the mandibular joint, toothache.</TARA:hasIndicationsDescription>
        <TARA:hasInnervationDescription>The branch of the facial nerve, the auriculotemporal nerve.</TARA:hasInnervationDescription>
        <TARA:hasLocationalDescription>On the face, in the depression between the anterior border of the centre of the tragus and the posterior border of the condylar process of the mandible. Note: When the mouth is slightly opened, SI19 is located in the depression anterior to the centre of the tragus, between tE21 and gB2.</TARA:hasLocationalDescription>
        <TARA:hasMeridian rdf:resource="http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_9093316"/>
        <TARA:hasMethodDescription>Puncture perpendicularly 0.5-1.0 inch when the mouth is open. Moxibustion is applicable.</TARA:hasMethodDescription>
        <TARA:hasPinyinLabel>Tinggong</TARA:hasPinyinLabel>
        <TARA:hasRelatedLocation rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/UBERON_0004657"/>
        <TARA:hasRelatedLocation rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/UBERON_0010887"/>
        <TARA:hasSurfaceLocation rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/UBERON_0001456"/>
        <TARA:hasSynonym>Small Intestine 19</TARA:hasSynonym>
        <TARA:hasVasculatureDescription>The auricular branches of the superficial temporal artery and vein</TARA:hasVasculatureDescription>
        <rdfs:label>SI 19</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_1787498 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_1787498">
        <owl:equivalentClass>
            <owl:Class>
                <owl:intersectionOf rdf:parseType="Collection">
                    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_4943316"/>
                    <owl:Restriction>
                        <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/hasSpecialPointDesignation"/>
                        <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_1375080"/>
                    </owl:Restriction>
                </owl:intersectionOf>
            </owl:Class>
        </owl:equivalentClass>
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_4943316"/>
        <dc:description>Represents the five Shu Points. Each of the twelve main meridians has, below the elbow or knee, five specific points, namely, Jing-Well, Ying-Spring, Shu-Stream, Jing-River and He-Sea, which are termed Five Shu points in general. They are situated in the above order from the distal end of extremities to the elbow or knee.</dc:description>
        <dcterms:bibliographicCitation>D. Liangyue, G. Yijun, H. Shuhui, et al. Chinese Acupuncture and Moxibustion. Revised ed. Beijing: Foreign Languages Press; 1999. Ch. 6, An Introduction to Acupunture Points, Sec. III, Specific Points. ISBN 978-7-119-01758-7.</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
        <TARA:hasDesignatedSpecialPointRole rdf:resource="http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_1375080"/>
        <rdfs:label>Shu Point</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


