        <dcterms:bibliographicCitation>Source for the Acupoint Location: WHO Standard Acupuncture Point Locations in the Western Pacific Region. World Health Organization. Regional Office for the Western Pacific, 2008. Page 76. ISBN 978-92-9061-248-7.

Source for Acupuncture Method, Vasculature, Innervation, and Indications:
D. Liangyue, G. Yijun, H. Shuhui, et al. Chinese Acupuncture and Moxibustion. Revised ed. Beijing: Foreign Languages Press; 1999. Chapter 7, Acupuncture Points of the Taiyin and Yangming Meridian. ISBN 978-7-119-01758-7.</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
        <TARA:hasChineseLabel>腹結(结)</TARA:hasChineseLabel>
        <TARA:hasIndicationsDescription>Pain around the umbilical region, abdominal distension, hernia, diarrhea, constipation.</TARA:hasIndicationsDescription>
        <TARA:hasInnervationDescription>The eleventh intercostal nerve.</TARA:hasInnervationDescription>
        <TARA:hasLocationalDescription>On the lower abdomen, 1.3 B-cun inferior to the centre of the umbilicus, 4 B-cun lateral to the anterior median line.</TARA:hasLocationalDescription>
        <TARA:hasMeridian rdf:resource="http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_4167850"/>
        <TARA:hasMethodDescription>Puncture perpendicularly 0.5-1.0 inch. Moxibustion is applicable.</TARA:hasMethodDescription>
        <TARA:hasPinyinLabel>Fujie</TARA:hasPinyinLabel>
        <TARA:hasRelatedLocation rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/UBERON_0007118"/>
        <TARA:hasRelatedLocation rdf:resource="http://uri.interlex.org/base/ilx_0795285"/>
        <TARA:hasSurfaceLocation rdf:resource="http://uri.interlex.org/base/ilx_0795303"/>
        <TARA:hasSynonym>Spleen 14</TARA:hasSynonym>
        <TARA:hasVasculatureDescription>The eleventh intercostal artery and vein.</TARA:hasVasculatureDescription>
        <rdfs:label>SP 14</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_2003524 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_2003524">
        <owl:equivalentClass>
            <owl:Class>
                <owl:intersectionOf rdf:parseType="Collection">
                    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_7010544"/>
                    <owl:Restriction>
                        <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/hasSpecialPointDesignation"/>
                        <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_4591653"/>
                    </owl:Restriction>
                </owl:intersectionOf>
            </owl:Class>
        </owl:equivalentClass>
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_7010544"/>
        <dc:description>The Eight Influential Points refer to the specific points which have particular effects in treatment of disorders relating to the zang-fu organs, qi, blood, tendon, pulse and vessels, bone and marrow. Each of the Eight Influential Points overlaps the other point. Yanglingquan (GB 34) is the Influential Point of tendons.</dc:description>
        <dcterms:bibliographicCitation>D. Liangyue, G. Yijun, H. Shuhui, et al. Chinese Acupuncture and Moxibustion. Revised ed. Beijing: Foreign Languages Press; 1999. Ch. 6, An Introduction to Acupunture Points, Sec. III, Specific Points. ISBN 978-7-119-01758-7.</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
        <TARA:hasDesignatedSpecialPointRole rdf:resource="http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_4591653"/>
        <rdfs:label>Influential Point of Tendon</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_2016600 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_2016600">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_0739106"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
