                    </owl:Restriction>
                </owl:someValuesFrom>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <dcterms:bibliographicCitation>Source for the Acupoint Location: WHO Standard Acupuncture Point Locations in the Western Pacific Region. World Health Organization. Regional Office for the Western Pacific, 2008. Page 181. ISBN 978-92-9061-248-7.

Source for Acupuncture Method, Vasculature, Innervation, and Indications:
D. Liangyue, G. Yijun, H. Shuhui, et al. Chinese Acupuncture and Moxibustion. Revised ed. Beijing: Foreign Languages Press; 1999. Chapter 9, Acupuncture Points of the Jueyin and Shaoyang Meridian. ISBN 978-7-119-01758-7.</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
        <TARA:hasChineseLabel>風(风)池</TARA:hasChineseLabel>
        <TARA:hasIndicationsDescription>Headache, vertigo, insomnia, pain and stiffness of the neck, blurred vision, glaucoma, red and painful eyes, tinnitus, convulsion, epilepsy, infantile convulsion, febrile diseases, common cold, nasal obstruction, rhinorrhea.</TARA:hasIndicationsDescription>
        <TARA:hasInnervationDescription>The branch of the lesser occipital nerve.</TARA:hasInnervationDescription>
        <TARA:hasLocationalDescription>In the anterior region of the neck, inferior to the occipital bone, in the depression between the origins of sternocleidomastoid and the trapezius muscles. Note: GB20 is at the same level as GV16.</TARA:hasLocationalDescription>
        <TARA:hasMeridian rdf:resource="http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_5283975"/>
        <TARA:hasMethodDescription>Puncture 0.5-0.8 inch towards the tip of the nose. Moxibustion is applicable.</TARA:hasMethodDescription>
        <TARA:hasPinyinLabel>Fengchi</TARA:hasPinyinLabel>
        <TARA:hasRelatedLocation rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/UBERON_0001128"/>
        <TARA:hasRelatedLocation rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/UBERON_0001676"/>
        <TARA:hasRelatedLocation rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/UBERON_0002380"/>
        <TARA:hasSurfaceLocation rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/UBERON_0000974"/>
        <TARA:hasSynonym>G 20</TARA:hasSynonym>
        <TARA:hasSynonym>Gallbladder 20</TARA:hasSynonym>
        <TARA:hasVasculatureDescription>The branches of the occipital artery and vein.</TARA:hasVasculatureDescription>
        <rdfs:label>GB 20</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_2358051 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_2358051">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_3535998"/>
        <dcterms:bibliographicCitation>D. Liangyue, G. Yijun, H. Shuhui, et al. Chinese Acupuncture and Moxibustion. Revised ed. Beijing: Foreign Languages Press; 1999. Ch. 10, Sec. III, The Extra Points. ISBN 978-7-119-01758-7.</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
        <TARA:hasIndicationsDescription>Alalia, salivation with stiff tongue, sore throat, difficulty in swallowing, loss of voice.</TARA:hasIndicationsDescription>
        <TARA:hasLocationalDescription>I cun below the midpoint of the lower jaw, in the depression between the hyoid bone and the lower border of the jaw.</TARA:hasLocationalDescription>
        <TARA:hasMethodDescription>Puncture obliquely 0.8-1.2 inches toward the tongue root.</TARA:hasMethodDescription>
        <TARA:hasSynonym>EX-HN 21</TARA:hasSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>Shanglianquan</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_2385378 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_2385378">
