                    </owl:Restriction>
                </owl:someValuesFrom>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <dcterms:bibliographicCitation>Source for the Acupoint Location: WHO Standard Acupuncture Point Locations in the Western Pacific Region. World Health Organization. Regional Office for the Western Pacific, 2008. Page 153. ISBN 978-92-9061-248-7.

Source for Acupuncture Method, Vasculature, Innervation, and Indications:
D. Liangyue, G. Yijun, H. Shuhui, et al. Chinese Acupuncture and Moxibustion. Revised ed. Beijing: Foreign Languages Press; 1999. Chapter 9, Acupuncture Points of the Jueyin and Shaoyang Meridian. ISBN 978-7-119-01758-7.</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
        <TARA:hasChineseLabel>郄門(门)</TARA:hasChineseLabel>
        <TARA:hasDesignatedSpecialPointRole rdf:resource="http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_4598552"/>
        <TARA:hasIndicationsDescription>Cardiac pain, palpitation, epistaxis, hematemesis, haemoptysis chest pain, furuncle, epilepsy.</TARA:hasIndicationsDescription>
        <TARA:hasInnervationDescription>The medial antebrachial cutaneous nerve; deeper, the median nerve; deepest, the anterior interosseous nerve.</TARA:hasInnervationDescription>
        <TARA:hasLocationalDescription>On the anterior aspect of the forearm, between the tendons of the palmaris longus and the flexor carpi radialis, 5 B-cun proximal to the palmar wrist crease. Note 1: With the fist clenched, the wrist supinated, and the elbow slightly flexed, the two tendons become more prominent. PC4 is located 1 B-cun distal to the midpoint of the line connecting PC3 with PC7. Note 2: If the palmaris longus tendon is not present, PC4 is medial to the flexor carpi radialis tendon.</TARA:hasLocationalDescription>
        <TARA:hasMeridian rdf:resource="http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_4694897"/>
        <TARA:hasMethodDescription>Puncture perpendicularly 0.5-1.0 inch. Moxibustion is applicable.</TARA:hasMethodDescription>
        <TARA:hasPinyinLabel>Ximen</TARA:hasPinyinLabel>
        <TARA:hasRelatedLocation rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/UBERON_0016496"/>
        <TARA:hasRelatedLocation rdf:resource="http://uri.interlex.org/base/ilx_0795334"/>
        <TARA:hasRelatedLocation rdf:resource="http://uri.interlex.org/base/ilx_0795500"/>
        <TARA:hasSurfaceLocation rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/UBERON_0002386"/>
        <TARA:hasSynonym>P 4</TARA:hasSynonym>
        <TARA:hasSynonym>Pericardium 4</TARA:hasSynonym>
        <TARA:hasVasculatureDescription>The median artery and vein; deeper, the anterior interosseous artery and vein.</TARA:hasVasculatureDescription>
        <rdfs:label>PC 4</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_2678362 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_2678362">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_8364824"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
