        <dcterms:bibliographicCitation>Source for the Acupoint Location: WHO Standard Acupuncture Point Locations in the Western Pacific Region. World Health Organization. Regional Office for the Western Pacific, 2008. Page 71. ISBN 978-92-9061-248-7.

Source for Acupuncture Method, Vasculature, Innervation, and Indications:
D. Liangyue, G. Yijun, H. Shuhui, et al. Chinese Acupuncture and Moxibustion. Revised ed. Beijing: Foreign Languages Press; 1999. Chapter 7, Acupuncture Points of the Taiyin and Yangming Meridian. ISBN 978-7-119-01758-7.</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
        <TARA:hasChineseLabel>太白</TARA:hasChineseLabel>
        <TARA:hasDesignatedSpecialPointRole rdf:resource="http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_1840369"/>
        <TARA:hasDesignatedSpecialPointRole rdf:resource="http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_6705086"/>
        <TARA:hasIndicationsDescription>Gastric pain, abdominal distension, constipation, dysentery, vomiting diarrhea, borborygmus, sluggishness, beriberi.</TARA:hasIndicationsDescription>
        <TARA:hasInnervationDescription>The branches of the saphenous nerve and superficial peroneal nerve.</TARA:hasInnervationDescription>
        <TARA:hasLocationalDescription>On the medial aspect of the foot, in the depression proximal to the first metatarsophalangeal joint, at the border between the red and white flesh.</TARA:hasLocationalDescription>
        <TARA:hasMeridian rdf:resource="http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_4167850"/>
        <TARA:hasMethodDescription>Puncture perpendicularly 0.3-0.5 inch. Moxibustion is applicable.</TARA:hasMethodDescription>
        <TARA:hasPinyinLabel>Taibai</TARA:hasPinyinLabel>
        <TARA:hasRelatedLocation rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/UBERON_0007750"/>
        <TARA:hasSurfaceLocation rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/UBERON_0002387"/>
        <TARA:hasSynonym>Spleen 3</TARA:hasSynonym>
        <TARA:hasVasculatureDescription>The dorsal venous network of the foot, the medial plantar artery and the branches of the medial tarsal artery.</TARA:hasVasculatureDescription>
        <rdfs:label>SP 3</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


