        <dcterms:bibliographicCitation>Source for the Acupoint Location: WHO Standard Acupuncture Point Locations in the Western Pacific Region. World Health Organization. Regional Office for the Western Pacific, 2008. Page 181. ISBN 978-92-9061-248-7.

Source for Acupuncture Method, Vasculature, Innervation, and Indications:
D. Liangyue, G. Yijun, H. Shuhui, et al. Chinese Acupuncture and Moxibustion. Revised ed. Beijing: Foreign Languages Press; 1999. Chapter 9, Acupuncture Points of the Jueyin and Shaoyang Meridian. ISBN 978-7-119-01758-7.</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
        <TARA:hasChineseLabel>腦(脑,脳)空</TARA:hasChineseLabel>
        <TARA:hasIndicationsDescription>Headache, stiffness of the neck, vertigo, painful eyes, tinnitus, epilepsy.</TARA:hasIndicationsDescription>
        <TARA:hasLocationalDescription>On the head, at the same level as the supe- rior border of the external occipital protu- berance, directly superior to gB20. Note: GB19 is at the same level as GV17 and BL9.</TARA:hasLocationalDescription>
        <TARA:hasMeridian rdf:resource="http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_5283975"/>
        <TARA:hasMethodDescription>Puncture subcutaneously 0.3-0.5 inch. Moxibustion is applicable.</TARA:hasMethodDescription>
        <TARA:hasPinyinLabel>Naokong</TARA:hasPinyinLabel>
        <TARA:hasRelatedLocation rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/UBERON_0013469"/>
        <TARA:hasSurfaceLocation rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/UBERON_0000033"/>
        <TARA:hasSynonym>G 19</TARA:hasSynonym>
        <TARA:hasSynonym>Gallbladder 19</TARA:hasSynonym>
        <TARA:hasVasculatureDescription>See Chengling (GB 18).</TARA:hasVasculatureDescription>
        <rdfs:label>GB 19</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


