D. Liangyue, G. Yijun, H. Shuhui, et al. Chinese Acupuncture and Moxibustion. Revised ed. Beijing: Foreign Languages Press; 1999. Chapter 7, Acupuncture Points of the Taiyin and Yangming Meridian. ISBN 978-7-119-01758-7.</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
        <TARA:hasChineseLabel>列缺</TARA:hasChineseLabel>
        <TARA:hasDesignatedSpecialPointRole rdf:resource="http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_6993030"/>
        <TARA:hasDesignatedSpecialPointRole rdf:resource="http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_9493899"/>
        <TARA:hasIndicationsDescription>Headache, migraine, neck rigidity, cough, asthma, sore throat, facial paralysis, toothache, pain and weakness of the wrist.</TARA:hasIndicationsDescription>
        <TARA:hasInnervationDescription>The lateral antebrachial cutaneous nerve and the superficial ramus of the radial nerve.</TARA:hasInnervationDescription>
        <TARA:hasLocationalDescription>On the radial aspect of the forearm, between the tendons of the abductor pollicis longus and the extensor pollicis brevis muscles, in the groove for the abductor pollicis longus tendon, 1.5 B-cun superior to the palmar wrist crease.</TARA:hasLocationalDescription>
        <TARA:hasMeridian rdf:resource="http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_9964503"/>
        <TARA:hasPinyinLabel>Lieque</TARA:hasPinyinLabel>
        <TARA:hasRelatedLocation rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/UBERON_0017618"/>
        <TARA:hasRelatedLocation rdf:resource="http://uri.interlex.org/base/ilx_0795334"/>
        <TARA:hasRelatedLocation rdf:resource="http://uri.interlex.org/base/ilx_0795335"/>
        <TARA:hasSurfaceLocation rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/UBERON_0002386"/>
        <TARA:hasSynonym>L 7</TARA:hasSynonym>
        <TARA:hasSynonym>Lung 7</TARA:hasSynonym>
        <TARA:hasVasculatureDescription>The cephalic vein, branches of the radial artery and vein.</TARA:hasVasculatureDescription>
        <rdfs:label>LU 7</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_3535998 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_3535998">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_1132428"/>
        <dc:description>Extra points are experiential points with specific names and definite locations, but are not attributed to the fourteen meridians. They are effective in the treatment of certain diseases. Although scattered over the body, they are still related to the meridians system. Some extra points are distributed along the courses of the fourteen meridians, for example, Yimang (Extra I) i; related to the Governor Vessel, Lanwei (Extra 18) to the Stomach Meridian of Foot-Yangming.</dc:description>
        <dcterms:bibliographicCitation>D. Liangyue, G. Yijun, H. Shuhui, et al. Chinese Acupuncture and Moxibustion. Revised ed. Beijing: Foreign Languages Press; 1999. Page 116. ISBN 978-7-119-01758-7.</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
        <TARA:hasSynonym>Extra Acupuncture Point</TARA:hasSynonym>
        <TARA:hasSynonym>Extra point</TARA:hasSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>Extra Acupoint</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_3549064 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_3549064">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_1926756"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/locatedOnTheSurfaceOf"/>
