                    </owl:Restriction>
                </owl:someValuesFrom>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <dcterms:bibliographicCitation>Source for the Acupoint Location: WHO Standard Acupuncture Point Locations in the Western Pacific Region. World Health Organization. Regional Office for the Western Pacific, 2008. Page 174. ISBN 978-92-9061-248-7.

Source for Acupuncture Method, Vasculature, Innervation, and Indications:
D. Liangyue, G. Yijun, H. Shuhui, et al. Chinese Acupuncture and Moxibustion. Revised ed. Beijing: Foreign Languages Press; 1999. Chapter 9, Acupuncture Points of the Jueyin and Shaoyang Meridian. ISBN 978-7-119-01758-7.</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
        <TARA:hasChineseLabel>懸(悬)顱(颅)</TARA:hasChineseLabel>
        <TARA:hasIndicationsDescription>Migraine, pain in the outer canthus, facial swelling.</TARA:hasIndicationsDescription>
        <TARA:hasLocationalDescription>On the head, at the midpoint of the curved line from St8 to gB7.</TARA:hasLocationalDescription>
        <TARA:hasMeridian rdf:resource="http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_5283975"/>
        <TARA:hasMethodDescription>Puncture subcutaneously 0.3-0.5 inch. Moxibustion is applicable.</TARA:hasMethodDescription>
        <TARA:hasPinyinLabel>Xuanlu</TARA:hasPinyinLabel>
        <TARA:hasSurfaceLocation rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/UBERON_0000033"/>
        <TARA:hasSynonym>G 5</TARA:hasSynonym>
        <TARA:hasSynonym>Gallbladder 5</TARA:hasSynonym>
        <TARA:hasVasculatureDescription>See Hanyan (GB 4).</TARA:hasVasculatureDescription>
        <rdfs:label>GB 5</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


