        <dcterms:bibliographicCitation>Source for the Acupoint Location: WHO Standard Acupuncture Point Locations in the Western Pacific Region. World Health Organization. Regional Office for the Western Pacific, 2008. Page 177. ISBN 978-92-9061-248-7.

Source for Acupuncture Method, Vasculature, Innervation, and Indications:
D. Liangyue, G. Yijun, H. Shuhui, et al. Chinese Acupuncture and Moxibustion. Revised ed. Beijing: Foreign Languages Press; 1999. Chapter 9, Acupuncture Points of the Jueyin and Shaoyang Meridian. ISBN 978-7-119-01758-7.</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
        <TARA:hasChineseLabel>頭(头)竅(窍)陰(阴)</TARA:hasChineseLabel>
        <TARA:hasIndicationsDescription>Pain of the head and neck, tinnitus, deafness, pain in the ears.</TARA:hasIndicationsDescription>
        <TARA:hasInnervationDescription>The anastomotic branch of the great and lesser occipital nerves.</TARA:hasInnervationDescription>
        <TARA:hasLocationalDescription>On the head, posterior and superior to the mastoid process, at the junction of the up- per two thirds and lower one third of the curved line from gB 9 to gB12.</TARA:hasLocationalDescription>
        <TARA:hasMeridian rdf:resource="http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_5283975"/>
        <TARA:hasMethodDescription>Puncture subcutaneously 0.3-0.5 inch. Moxibustion is applicable.</TARA:hasMethodDescription>
        <TARA:hasPinyinLabel>Touqiaoyin</TARA:hasPinyinLabel>
        <TARA:hasRelatedLocation rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/UBERON_0011220"/>
        <TARA:hasSurfaceLocation rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/UBERON_0000033"/>
        <TARA:hasSynonym>G 11</TARA:hasSynonym>
        <TARA:hasSynonym>Gallbladder 11</TARA:hasSynonym>
        <TARA:hasVasculatureDescription>The branches of the posterior auricular artery and vein.</TARA:hasVasculatureDescription>
        <rdfs:label>GB 11</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


