Source for Acupuncture Method, Vasculature, Innervation, and Indications:
D. Liangyue, G. Yijun, H. Shuhui, et al. Chinese Acupuncture and Moxibustion. Revised ed. Beijing: Foreign Languages Press; 1999. Chapter 7, Acupuncture Points of the Taiyin and Yangming Meridian. ISBN 978-7-119-01758-7.</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
        <TARA:hasChineseLabel>天樞(枢)</TARA:hasChineseLabel>
        <TARA:hasDesignatedSpecialPointRole rdf:resource="http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_1468221"/>
        <TARA:hasIndicationsDescription>Abdominal pain and distension, borborygmus, pain around the umbilicus, constipation, diarrhea, dysentery, irregular menstruation, edema.</TARA:hasIndicationsDescription>
        <TARA:hasInnervationDescription>The branch of the tenth intercostal nerve.</TARA:hasInnervationDescription>
        <TARA:hasLocationalDescription>On the upper abdomen, 2 B-cun lateral to the centre of the umbilicus.</TARA:hasLocationalDescription>
        <TARA:hasMeridian rdf:resource="http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_6221269"/>
        <TARA:hasMethodDescription>Puncture perpendicularly 0.7-1.2 inches. Moxibustion is applicable.</TARA:hasMethodDescription>
