        <dcterms:bibliographicCitation>Source for the Acupoint Location: WHO Standard Acupuncture Point Locations in the Western Pacific Region. World Health Organization. Regional Office for the Western Pacific, 2008. Page 93. ISBN 978-92-9061-248-7.

Source for Acupuncture Method, Vasculature, Innervation, and Indications:
D. Liangyue, G. Yijun, H. Shuhui, et al. Chinese Acupuncture and Moxibustion. Revised ed. Beijing: Foreign Languages Press; 1999. Chapter 8, Acupuncture Points of the Shaoyin and Taiyang Meridian. ISBN 978-7-119-01758-7.</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
        <TARA:hasChineseLabel>秉風(风)</TARA:hasChineseLabel>
        <TARA:hasIndicationsDescription>Pain in the scapular region, numbness and aching of the upper extremities, motor impairment of the shoulder and arm.</TARA:hasIndicationsDescription>
        <TARA:hasInnervationDescription>The lateral suprascapular nerve and accessory nerve; deeper, the suprascapular nerve.</TARA:hasInnervationDescription>
        <TARA:hasLocationalDescription>In the scapular region, in the supraspinatous fossa, superior to the midpoint of the spine of the scapula.</TARA:hasLocationalDescription>
        <TARA:hasMeridian rdf:resource="http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_9093316"/>
        <TARA:hasMethodDescription>Puncture perpendicularly 0.5-0.7 inch. Moxibustion is applicable.</TARA:hasMethodDescription>
        <TARA:hasPinyinLabel>Bingfeng</TARA:hasPinyinLabel>
        <TARA:hasRelatedLocation rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/UBERON_0004651"/>
        <TARA:hasRelatedLocation rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/UBERON_0006849"/>
        <TARA:hasSurfaceLocation rdf:resource="http://uri.interlex.org/base/ilx_0795319"/>
        <TARA:hasSynonym>Small Intestine 12</TARA:hasSynonym>
        <TARA:hasVasculatureDescription>The suprascapular artery and vein.</TARA:hasVasculatureDescription>
        <rdfs:label>SI 12</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


