        <dcterms:bibliographicCitation>Source for the Acupoint Location: WHO Standard Acupuncture Point Locations in the Western Pacific Region. World Health Organization. Regional Office for the Western Pacific, 2008. Page 139. ISBN 978-92-9061-248-7.

Source for Acupuncture Method, Vasculature, Innervation, and Indications:
D. Liangyue, G. Yijun, H. Shuhui, et al. Chinese Acupuncture and Moxibustion. Revised ed. Beijing: Foreign Languages Press; 1999. Chapter 8, Acupuncture Points of the Shaoyin and Taiyang Meridian. ISBN 978-7-119-01758-7.</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
        <TARA:hasChineseLabel>交信</TARA:hasChineseLabel>
        <TARA:hasDesignatedSpecialPointRole rdf:resource="http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_7524444"/>
        <TARA:hasIndicationsDescription>Irregular menstruation, dysmenorrhea, uterine bleeding, prolapse of uterus, diarrhea, constipation, pain and swelling of testis.</TARA:hasIndicationsDescription>
        <TARA:hasInnervationDescription>The medial crural cutaneous nerve; deeper, the tibial nerve.</TARA:hasInnervationDescription>
        <TARA:hasLocationalDescription>On the medial aspect of the leg, in the depression posterior to the medial border of the tibial bone, 2 B-cun superior to the prominence of the medial malleolus. Note: 0.5 B-cun anterior to KI7.</TARA:hasLocationalDescription>
        <TARA:hasMeridian rdf:resource="http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_4813389"/>
