        <dcterms:bibliographicCitation>Source for the Acupoint Location: WHO Standard Acupuncture Point Locations in the Western Pacific Region. World Health Organization. Regional Office for the Western Pacific, 2008. Page 43. ISBN 978-92-9061-248-7.

Source for Acupuncture Method, Vasculature, Innervation, and Indications:
D. Liangyue, G. Yijun, H. Shuhui, et al. Chinese Acupuncture and Moxibustion. Revised ed. Beijing: Foreign Languages Press; 1999. Chapter 7, Acupuncture Points of the Taiyin and Yangming Meridian. ISBN 978-7-119-01758-7.</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
        <TARA:hasChineseLabel>迎(迎)香</TARA:hasChineseLabel>
        <TARA:hasIndicationsDescription>Nasal obstruction, hyposmia, epistaxis, rhinorrhea, deviation of the mouth, itching and swelling of the face.</TARA:hasIndicationsDescription>
        <TARA:hasInnervationDescription>The anastomotic branch of the facial nerve and infraorbital nerves.</TARA:hasInnervationDescription>
        <TARA:hasLocationalDescription>On the face, in the nasolabial sulcus, at the same level as the midpoint of lateral border of the ala of the nose. Remarks: Alternative location for LI20 - On the face, in the nasolabial sulcus, at the level of the inferior border of the ala of the nose.</TARA:hasLocationalDescription>
        <TARA:hasMeridian rdf:resource="http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_4610474"/>
        <TARA:hasMethodDescription>Puncture obliquely or subcutaneously 0.3-0.5 inch.</TARA:hasMethodDescription>
        <TARA:hasPinyinLabel>Yingxiang</TARA:hasPinyinLabel>
        <TARA:hasRelatedLocation rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/UBERON_0006906"/>
        <TARA:hasRelatedLocation rdf:resource="http://uri.interlex.org/base/ilx_0795290"/>
        <TARA:hasSurfaceLocation rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/UBERON_0001456"/>
        <TARA:hasSynonym>Large Intestine 20</TARA:hasSynonym>
        <TARA:hasVasculatureDescription>The facial artery and vein, the branches of the infraorbital artery and vein.</TARA:hasVasculatureDescription>
        <rdfs:label>LI 20</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_6705086 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_6705086">
        <owl:equivalentClass>
            <owl:Class>
                <owl:intersectionOf rdf:parseType="Collection">
                    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_1787498"/>
                    <owl:Restriction>
                        <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/hasSpecialPointDesignation"/>
                        <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_1716565"/>
                    </owl:Restriction>
                </owl:intersectionOf>
            </owl:Class>
        </owl:equivalentClass>
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_1787498"/>
        <dc:description>The names of the five shu points image the flow of meridian q i as the flow of water. The Shu-Stream point is where the meridian qi flourishes. In the yin meridians, the Yuan-Primary Poims overlap with the Shu-Stream Points of the Five Shu Points. Each yang meridian, however, has its Yuan-Primary Point other than the Shu-Stream Point.</dc:description>
        <dcterms:bibliographicCitation>D. Liangyue, G. Yijun, H. Shuhui, et al. Chinese Acupuncture and Moxibustion. Revised ed. Beijing: Foreign Languages Press; 1999. Ch. 6, An Introduction to Acupunture Points, Sec. III, Specific Points. ISBN 978-7-119-01758-7.</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
        <TARA:hasDesignatedSpecialPointRole rdf:resource="http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_1716565"/>
        <rdfs:label>Shu-Stream Point</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


