                        <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/BFO_0000050"/>
                        <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://uri.interlex.org/base/ilx_0795303"/>
                    </owl:Restriction>
                </owl:someValuesFrom>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <dcterms:bibliographicCitation>Source for the Acupoint Location: WHO Standard Acupuncture Point Locations in the Western Pacific Region. World Health Organization. Regional Office for the Western Pacific, 2008. Page 220. ISBN 978-92-9061-248-7.

Source for Acupuncture Method, Vasculature, Innervation, and Indications:
D. Liangyue, G. Yijun, H. Shuhui, et al. Chinese Acupuncture and Moxibustion. Revised ed. Beijing: Foreign Languages Press; 1999. Chapter 10, Acupuncture Points of the Governor and the Conception Vessels and the Extra Points. ISBN 978-7-119-01758-7.</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
        <TARA:hasChineseLabel>曲骨(骨)</TARA:hasChineseLabel>
        <TARA:hasIndicationsDescription>Retention and dribbling of urine, enuresis, nocturnal emission, impotence, morbid leukorrhea, irregular menstruation, dysmenorrhea, hernia.</TARA:hasIndicationsDescription>
        <TARA:hasInnervationDescription>The branch of the iliohypogastric nerve.</TARA:hasInnervationDescription>
        <TARA:hasLocationalDescription>On the lower abdomen, superior to the pubic symphysis, on the anterior median line.</TARA:hasLocationalDescription>
        <TARA:hasMeridian rdf:resource="http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_6789717"/>
        <TARA:hasMethodDescription>Puncture perpendicularly 0.5-1.0 inch. Great care should be taken to puncture the points from Qugu (CV 2) to Shangwan (CV 13) of this meridian in pregnant women. Moxibustion is applicable.</TARA:hasMethodDescription>
        <TARA:hasPinyinLabel>Qugu</TARA:hasPinyinLabel>
        <TARA:hasRelatedLocation rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/UBERON_0003699"/>
        <TARA:hasRelatedLocation rdf:resource="http://uri.interlex.org/base/ilx_0795285"/>
        <TARA:hasSurfaceLocation rdf:resource="http://uri.interlex.org/base/ilx_0795303"/>
        <TARA:hasSynonym>Conception Vessel 2</TARA:hasSynonym>
        <TARA:hasSynonym>RN 2</TARA:hasSynonym>
        <TARA:hasSynonym>Ren 2</TARA:hasSynonym>
        <TARA:hasVasculatureDescription>The branches of the inferior epigastric artery and the obturator artery.</TARA:hasVasculatureDescription>
        <rdfs:label>CV 2</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_6993030 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_6993030">
        <owl:equivalentClass>
            <owl:Class>
                <owl:intersectionOf rdf:parseType="Collection">
                    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_4943316"/>
                    <owl:Restriction>
                        <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/hasSpecialPointDesignation"/>
                        <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_2162578"/>
                    </owl:Restriction>
                </owl:intersectionOf>
            </owl:Class>
        </owl:equivalentClass>
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_4943316"/>
        <dc:description>Each of twelve main meridians has, on the limbs, a Luo-Connecting Point to link its exteriorly-interiorly related meridian. Each of the Governor and Conception vessels., and the Major Collateral of the spleen has its Luo-Connecting Point on the trunk. They are termed &quot;the Fifteen Luo-Connecting Points&quot;. A Luo-Connecting Point is used to treat disorders involving the two exteriorly-interiorly related meridians and those in the area supplied by the two meridians.</dc:description>
        <dcterms:bibliographicCitation>D. Liangyue, G. Yijun, H. Shuhui, et al. Chinese Acupuncture and Moxibustion. Revised ed. Beijing: Foreign Languages Press; 1999. Ch. 6, An Introduction to Acupunture Points, Sec. III, Specific Points. ISBN 978-7-119-01758-7.</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
        <TARA:hasDesignatedSpecialPointRole rdf:resource="http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_2162578"/>
        <rdfs:label>Luo-Connecting Point</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_7010544 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_7010544">
        <owl:equivalentClass>
            <owl:Class>
                <owl:intersectionOf rdf:parseType="Collection">
                    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_7852472"/>
                    <owl:Restriction>
                        <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/hasSpecialPointDesignation"/>
                        <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_4235924"/>
                    </owl:Restriction>
                </owl:intersectionOf>
            </owl:Class>
        </owl:equivalentClass>
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_7852472"/>
        <dc:description>The Eight Influential Points refer to the specific points which have particular effects in treatment of disorders relating to the zang-fu organs, qi, blood, tendon, pulse and vessels, bone and marrow. Each of the Eight Influential Points overlaps the other point.</dc:description>
        <dcterms:bibliographicCitation>D. Liangyue, G. Yijun, H. Shuhui, et al. Chinese Acupuncture and Moxibustion. Revised ed. Beijing: Foreign Languages Press; 1999. Ch. 6, An Introduction to Acupunture Points, Sec. III, Specific Points. ISBN 978-7-119-01758-7.</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
        <TARA:hasDesignatedSpecialPointRole rdf:resource="http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_4235924"/>
        <rdfs:label>Influential Point</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_7083740 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_7083740">
        <owl:equivalentClass>
            <owl:Class>
                <owl:intersectionOf rdf:parseType="Collection">
                    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_7010544"/>
                    <owl:Restriction>
                        <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/hasSpecialPointDesignation"/>
                        <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_5906686"/>
                    </owl:Restriction>
                </owl:intersectionOf>
            </owl:Class>
        </owl:equivalentClass>
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_7010544"/>
        <dc:description>The Eight Influential Points refer to the specific points which have particular effects in treatment of disorders relating to the zang-fu organs, qi, blood, tendon, pulse and vessels, bone and marrow. Each of the Eight Influential Points overlaps the other point. Zhongwan (CV 12) is the Influential Point of the fu organs. Zhongwan (CV 12), the Influential Point of the fu organs, is selected to lower the qi of the fu organ.</dc:description>
        <dcterms:bibliographicCitation>D. Liangyue, G. Yijun, H. Shuhui, et al. Chinese Acupuncture and Moxibustion. Revised ed. Beijing: Foreign Languages Press; 1999. Ch. 6, An Introduction to Acupunture Points, Sec. III, Specific Points. ISBN 978-7-119-01758-7.</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
        <TARA:hasDesignatedSpecialPointRole rdf:resource="http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_5906686"/>
        <rdfs:label>Influential Point of the Fu Organs</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_7154478 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_7154478">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_2089647"/>
        <rdfs:label>The Lower He-Sea Point of the Small Intestine Role</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


