        <dcterms:bibliographicCitation>Source for the Acupoint Location: WHO Standard Acupuncture Point Locations in the Western Pacific Region. World Health Organization. Regional Office for the Western Pacific, 2008. Page 71. ISBN 978-92-9061-248-7.

Source for Acupuncture Method, Vasculature, Innervation, and Indications:
D. Liangyue, G. Yijun, H. Shuhui, et al. Chinese Acupuncture and Moxibustion. Revised ed. Beijing: Foreign Languages Press; 1999. Chapter 7, Acupuncture Points of the Taiyin and Yangming Meridian. ISBN 978-7-119-01758-7.</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
        <TARA:hasChineseLabel>公孫(孙)</TARA:hasChineseLabel>
        <TARA:hasDesignatedSpecialPointRole rdf:resource="http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_6993030"/>
        <TARA:hasDesignatedSpecialPointRole rdf:resource="http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_9493899"/>
        <TARA:hasIndicationsDescription>Gastric pain, vomiting, abdominal pain and distension, diarrhea, dysentery, borborygmus.</TARA:hasIndicationsDescription>
        <TARA:hasInnervationDescription>The saphenous nerve and the branch of the superficial peroneal nerve.</TARA:hasInnervationDescription>
        <TARA:hasLocationalDescription>On the medial aspect of the foot, antero- inferior to the base of the first metatarsal bone, at the border between the red and white flesh. Note: A depression can be felt when mov- ing proximally from SP3. SP4 is located in the depression distal to the base of the first metatarsal bone.</TARA:hasLocationalDescription>
        <TARA:hasMeridian rdf:resource="http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_4167850"/>
        <TARA:hasMethodDescription>Puncture perpendicularly 0.5-0.8 inch. Moxibustion is applicable.</TARA:hasMethodDescription>
        <TARA:hasPinyinLabel>Gongsun</TARA:hasPinyinLabel>
        <TARA:hasRelatedLocation rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/UBERON_0003650"/>
        <TARA:hasSurfaceLocation rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/UBERON_0002387"/>
        <TARA:hasSynonym>Spleen 4</TARA:hasSynonym>
        <TARA:hasVasculatureDescription>The medial tarsal artery and the dorsal venous network of the foot.</TARA:hasVasculatureDescription>
        <rdfs:label>SP 4</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_7423288 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_7423288">
