Source for Acupuncture Method, Vasculature, Innervation, and Indications:
D. Liangyue, G. Yijun, H. Shuhui, et al. Chinese Acupuncture and Moxibustion. Revised ed. Beijing: Foreign Languages Press; 1999. Chapter 10, Acupuncture Points of the Governor and the Conception Vessels and the Extra Points. ISBN 978-7-119-01758-7.</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
        <TARA:hasChineseLabel>上星</TARA:hasChineseLabel>
        <TARA:hasIndicationsDescription>Headache, ophthalmalgia, epistaxis. rhinorrhea, mental disorders.</TARA:hasIndicationsDescription>
        <TARA:hasInnervationDescription>The branch of the frontal nerve.</TARA:hasInnervationDescription>
        <TARA:hasLocationalDescription>On the head, 1 B-cun superior to the anterior hairline, on the anterior median line.</TARA:hasLocationalDescription>
        <TARA:hasMeridian rdf:resource="http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_0386099"/>
        <TARA:hasMethodDescription>Puncture subcutaneously 0.3-0.5 inch or prick to cause bleeding. This point is prohibited in infants with metopism. Moxibustion is applicable.</TARA:hasMethodDescription>
