        <dcterms:bibliographicCitation>Source for the Acupoint Location: WHO Standard Acupuncture Point Locations in the Western Pacific Region. World Health Organization. Regional Office for the Western Pacific, 2008. Page 205. ISBN 978-92-9061-248-7.

Source for Acupuncture Method, Vasculature, Innervation, and Indications:
D. Liangyue, G. Yijun, H. Shuhui, et al. Chinese Acupuncture and Moxibustion. Revised ed. Beijing: Foreign Languages Press; 1999. Chapter 10, Acupuncture Points of the Governor and the Conception Vessels and the Extra Points. ISBN 978-7-119-01758-7.</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
        <TARA:hasChineseLabel>命門(门)</TARA:hasChineseLabel>
        <TARA:hasIndicationsDescription>Stiffness of the back, lumbago, impotence, nocturnal emission, irregular menstruation, diarrhea, indigestion, leukorrhea.</TARA:hasIndicationsDescription>
        <TARA:hasLocationalDescription>In the lumbar region, in the depression inferior to the spinous process of the second lumbar vertebra (L2), on the posterior median line.</TARA:hasLocationalDescription>
        <TARA:hasMeridian rdf:resource="http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_0386099"/>
        <TARA:hasMethodDescription>Puncture perpendicularly 0.5-1.0 inch. Moxibustion is applicable.</TARA:hasMethodDescription>
        <TARA:hasPinyinLabel>Mingmen</TARA:hasPinyinLabel>
        <TARA:hasRelatedLocation rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/UBERON_0004618"/>
        <TARA:hasRelatedLocation rdf:resource="http://uri.interlex.org/base/ilx_0795312"/>
        <TARA:hasSurfaceLocation rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/UBERON_0005462"/>
        <TARA:hasSynonym>Du 4</TARA:hasSynonym>
        <TARA:hasSynonym>Governor Vessel 4</TARA:hasSynonym>
        <TARA:hasSynonym>Mingmen</TARA:hasSynonym>
        <TARA:hasVasculatureDescription>See Yaoyangguan (GY 3).</TARA:hasVasculatureDescription>
        <rdfs:label>GV 4</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


