        <dcterms:bibliographicCitation>Source for the Acupoint Location: WHO Standard Acupuncture Point Locations in the Western Pacific Region. World Health Organization. Regional Office for the Western Pacific, 2008. Page 140. ISBN 978-92-9061-248-7.

Source for Acupuncture Method, Vasculature, Innervation, and Indications:
D. Liangyue, G. Yijun, H. Shuhui, et al. Chinese Acupuncture and Moxibustion. Revised ed. Beijing: Foreign Languages Press; 1999. Chapter 8, Acupuncture Points of the Shaoyin and Taiyang Meridian. ISBN 978-7-119-01758-7.</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
        <TARA:hasChineseLabel>陰(阴)谷</TARA:hasChineseLabel>
        <TARA:hasDesignatedSpecialPointRole rdf:resource="http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_6594221"/>
        <TARA:hasIndicationsDescription>Impotence, hernia, uterine bleeding, dysuria, pain in the knee and popliteal fossa, mental disorders.</TARA:hasIndicationsDescription>
        <TARA:hasInnervationDescription>The medial femoral cutaneous nerve.</TARA:hasInnervationDescription>
        <TARA:hasLocationalDescription>On the posteromedial aspect of the knee, just lateral to the semitendinosus tendon, in the popliteal crease.</TARA:hasLocationalDescription>
        <TARA:hasMeridian rdf:resource="http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_4813389"/>
