        <dcterms:bibliographicCitation>Source for the Acupoint Location: WHO Standard Acupuncture Point Locations in the Western Pacific Region. World Health Organization. Regional Office for the Western Pacific, 2008. Page 185. ISBN 978-92-9061-248-7.

Source for Acupuncture Method, Vasculature, Innervation, and Indications:
D. Liangyue, G. Yijun, H. Shuhui, et al. Chinese Acupuncture and Moxibustion. Revised ed. Beijing: Foreign Languages Press; 1999. Chapter 9, Acupuncture Points of the Jueyin and Shaoyang Meridian. ISBN 978-7-119-01758-7.</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
        <TARA:hasChineseLabel>五樞(枢)</TARA:hasChineseLabel>
        <TARA:hasIndicationsDescription>Leukorrhea, lower abdominal pain, lumbar pain, hernia, constipation.</TARA:hasIndicationsDescription>
        <TARA:hasInnervationDescription>The iliohypogastric nerve.</TARA:hasInnervationDescription>
        <TARA:hasLocationalDescription>On the lower abdomen, 3 B-cun inferior to the centre of umbilicus, medial to the anterior superior iliac spine. Note: GB 27 is 3 B-cun inferior to GB 26, at the same level as CV 4.</TARA:hasLocationalDescription>
        <TARA:hasMeridian rdf:resource="http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_5283975"/>
        <TARA:hasMethodDescription>Puncture perpendicularly 0.5-1.0 inch. Moxibustion is applicable.</TARA:hasMethodDescription>
        <TARA:hasPinyinLabel>Wushu</TARA:hasPinyinLabel>
        <TARA:hasRelatedLocation rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/UBERON_0007118"/>
        <TARA:hasRelatedLocation rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/UBERON_0013708"/>
        <TARA:hasSurfaceLocation rdf:resource="http://uri.interlex.org/base/ilx_0795303"/>
        <TARA:hasSynonym>G 27</TARA:hasSynonym>
        <TARA:hasSynonym>Gallbladder 27</TARA:hasSynonym>
        <TARA:hasVasculatureDescription>The superficial and deep circumflex iliac arteries and veins.</TARA:hasVasculatureDescription>
        <rdfs:label>GB 27</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


