Source for Acupuncture Method, Vasculature, Innervation, and Indications:
D. Liangyue, G. Yijun, H. Shuhui, et al. Chinese Acupuncture and Moxibustion. Revised ed. Beijing: Foreign Languages Press; 1999. Chapter 7, Acupuncture Points of the Taiyin and Yangming Meridian. ISBN 978-7-119-01758-7.</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
        <TARA:hasChineseLabel>隱(隐,隠)白</TARA:hasChineseLabel>
        <TARA:hasDesignatedSpecialPointRole rdf:resource="http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_2954517"/>
        <TARA:hasIndicationsDescription>Abdominal distension, bloody stools, menorrhagia, uterine bleeding, mental disorders, dream disturbed sleep, convulsion.</TARA:hasIndicationsDescription>
        <TARA:hasInnervationDescription>On the anastomosis of the dorsal digital nerve derived from the superficial peroneal nerve and the plantar digital proprial nerve.</TARA:hasInnervationDescription>
        <TARA:hasLocationalDescription>On the great toe, medial to the distal phalanx, 0.1 f-cun proximal-medial to the me- dial corner of the toenail, at the intersection of the vertical line of the medial border and horizontal line of the base of the toenail.</TARA:hasLocationalDescription>
        <TARA:hasMeridian rdf:resource="http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_4167850"/>
        <TARA:hasMethodDescription>Puncture subcutaneously 0.1 inch. Moxibustion is applicable.</TARA:hasMethodDescription>
        <TARA:hasPinyinLabel>Yinbai</TARA:hasPinyinLabel>
